From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] schedsvc, explorer: a person's scheduled tasks run in their session

The Task Scheduler service runs as SYSTEM (patch 1172), so it left alone
the tasks registered for a particular person -- what programs installed
for one person register: an updater set to run at that person's log-on
or every day, a browser installed without administrator rights. Those
never ran.

A person's tasks now run in their own session, as them. The shell starts
"rundll32 schedsvc.dll,SgUserTaskHost" at each sign-in (once, with the
other programs that start at sign-in): the same scheduler, taking the
tasks whose principal is that person's account (its SID, name or
DOMAIN\name) or a group every person is in (Users, INTERACTIVE,
Authenticated Users, Everyone). Their log-on triggers fire at that
sign-in, time and calendar triggers when due; their last run times are
kept in the person's HKCU. One host per account in a session. Run on such
a task (asked of the service) is passed on to the person's host: the
service notes the request under HKLM\Software\Wine\Task Scheduler\Requests
and sets the event Global\SgTaskHostWake for two seconds; each host takes
the requests for its own tasks once. --as ACCOUNT names the account whose
tasks the host takes (they still run as whoever runs it), for tests.

Also: a log-on, boot or registration trigger that fired while its time
was already past (the host starts at the sign-in it reacts to) is now run
instead of being skipped.

Test: test/taskrun-gate.sh (mutants SG_MUTANT_TASK_NO_USER_HOST in
taskrun.c, SG_MUTANT_NO_USER_TASKS in explorer's startup.c).
